  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Football for Water 2.0 (2017 - 2019)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">During F4W 2.0 we aim to:- reach an additional 86.275 pupils, teachers and community members in and around 160 additional schools with our complete Football for Water 2.0 approach (ToC).- reach approximately 292.920 children,teachers and community members through improvement and facilitating growth in phase 1 schools- to ensure 10 years sustainability on O&amp;M in all Football for Water schools (total of approximately 522.000 children)</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Football for Water 2.0 is a follow-up program of Football for Water 1.0 that ended in 2016. A key hypothesis of the program is that the combination of sports and WASH brings lasting and sustainable improvements to children&#x2019;s hygiene and health seeking behavior. To be able to deliver lasting and durable results and funding, the F4W 2.0 focusses on:- Enhancement of local ownership of the &#x201c;game change&#x201d; that is taking place. This means local programs with local partners, reacting to local needs and opportunities.- Ensure institutionalization &amp; embedding. The F4W program should link to national systems, local communities and other stakeholders, as it must pertain to and/or improve standards, procedures and regulations as much as possible.- Sustain and expand behavior change with a continuum of integrated F4W life skills training both in the schools and in the community.- Focus on a sustainability approach towards Operation and Maintenance (O&amp;M) of WASH and sports facilities, through continuous monitoring of functionality and sound Cost Recovery Planning and involvement of/collaboration with the private sector.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">School children with a special focus on girls and surrounding communities</narrative>
  </description>
